<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch9-section-isobaric-coordinates-subsection-pgf" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Pressure gradient acceleration</title>

    <p>
        Terms in the equations of motion representing the horizontal components of the pressure gradient acceleration
        were found by holding <m>z</m> constant and allowing <m>p</m> to vary.
        They must be rewritten now that <m>p</m> is held constant in isobaric coordinates while <m>z</m> varies.
    </p>

    <p>
        We replace <m>f</m> with <m>p</m>
        in <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-x-p-reduced" text="type-global" />
        to write the <m>x</m>-component of the pressure gradient acceleration in isobaric coordinates.
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-chain-rule-p-p-reduced" number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            +
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            \right)_{x}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        But <m>\left( \dfrac{\partial p}{\partial x} \right)_{p} = 0</m> since <m>p</m> does not change on an isobaric surface,
        and so <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-p-p-reduced" text="type-global" /> reduces to
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-chain-rule-p-p-simplified" number="yes">
            0
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            +
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            \right)_{x}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        This rearranges to produce the relationship
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-chain-rule-p-p-rearranged" number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            -\left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            \right)_{x}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        If we apply hydrostatic balance
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            \frac{\partial p}{\partial z} = -\rho g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        and we note this could be written as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            \left( \frac{\partial p}{\partial z} \right)_x = -\rho g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        since <m>x</m> (and <m>y</m> and <m>t</m>)
        is held constant when finding the partial derivative with respect to <m>z</m>,
        we can rewrite <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-p-p-rearranged" text="type-global" /> further.
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-x-start" number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            -\left(
                -\rho g
            \right)
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
            =
            \rho g
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Furthermore, since the definition of geopotential gives <m>\partial \Phi = g \partial z</m>,
        we have
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-x-end" number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            \rho
            \left(
                \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Likewise,
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-y-end" number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            \rho g
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}
            \right)_{p}
            =
            \rho
            \left(
                \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial y}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        It follows from
        <xref ref="eqn-pga-isobaric-x-start" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-pga-isobaric-y-end" text="type-global" />
        that the horizontal components of the pressure gradient acceleration are given in isobaric coordinates as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-x" number="yes">
            -\frac{1}{\rho}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            -g
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
            =
            -\left(
                \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-y" number="yes">
            -\frac{1}{\rho}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            -g
            \left(
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}
            \right)_{p}
            =
            -\left(
                \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial y}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-vector" number="yes">
            -\frac{1}{\rho} \nabla_z p = -\nabla_p \Phi
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where the subscript "z" indicates that <m>z</m> is used as the vertical coordinate
        and the subscript "p" indicates that <m>p</m> is used as the vertical coordinate.
        In other words,
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-horizontal-gradient-operator-z" number="yes">
            \nabla_z
            =
            \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \right)_z \hat{i}
            +
            \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \right)_z \hat{j}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        is the horizontal gradient operator on a constant geometric height surface and
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-horizontal-gradient-operator-p" number="yes">
            \nabla_p
            =
            \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \right)_p \hat{i}
            +
            \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \right)_p \hat{j}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        is the horizontal gradient operator on an isobaric surface.
    </p>

    <p>
        Note that density <m>\rho</m> no longer appears in
        <xref ref="eqn-pga-isobaric-x" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-pga-isobaric-vector" text="type-global" />.
        This is an advantage of using isobaric coordinates:
        Equations are simplified,
        and we no longer need to consider density,
        which is not a commonly measured atmospheric variable.
    </p>

    <p>
        Since geopotential height is defined as <m>Z = \dfrac{\Phi}{g_0}</m>
        and <m>g_0</m> is constant,
        it is possible to write
        <xref ref="eqn-pga-isobaric-x" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-pga-isobaric-vector" text="type-global" />
        in terms of <m>Z</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-x-geopotential-height" number="yes">
            -\frac{1}{\rho}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            -\frac{g_0}{g_0}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
            = -g_0
            \left(
                \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \left( \frac{\Phi}{g_0} \right)
            \right)_p
            =
            -g_0
            \left(
                \frac{\partial Z}{\partial x}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-y-geopotential-height" number="yes">
            -\frac{1}{\rho}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
            \right)_{z}
            =
            -\frac{g_0}{g_0}
            \left(
                \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial y}
            \right)_{p}
            = -g_0
            \left(
                \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \left( \frac{\Phi}{g_0} \right)
            \right)_p
            =
            -g_0
            \left(
                \frac{\partial Z}{\partial y}
            \right)_{p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-pga-isobaric-vector-geopotential-height" number="yes">
            -\frac{1}{\rho} \nabla_z p = -g_0 \nabla_p Z
        </md>
    </p>

</subsection>